  Two decisions inside this that are doing real work:

  [1mThe storage functions are async — they return Promises — even though [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is synchronous.[0m This looks like
  pointless ceremony today and it's the single highest-value line in the design. In cycle 2 those functions become HTTP
  calls, which are unavoidably async. If cycle 1 writes them synchronously, every call site up the stack has to change
  when the backend arrives, which is exactly the rewrite we chose this approach to avoid. Paying for [38;5;153mawait[39m now makes the
  cycle 2 swap a one-file change.

  [1m[38;5;153mtasks.js[39m is the only module that calls [38;5;153mstorage.js[39m.[0m This is the write-path chokepoint we agreed on in the
  decomposition. It costs nothing now — it's just where the functions live — and it's what makes cycle 3's event log a
  change to one file rather than an audit of every handler. [38;5;153mrender.js[39m and [38;5;153mmain.js[39m never touch storage, so there's no
  back door to maintain later.

  Data flows one way: event → [38;5;153mtasks.js[39m → [38;5;153mstorage.js[39m → re-render from state. No two-way binding, no DOM as the source of
  truth.

  Alongside this, the tooling question — cheapest to answer now, before any code exists, since it lands in the spec's
  Global Constraints and every later cycle inherits it.
